<section class="phero"><img class="phero-bg" src="/assets/img/photos/consult.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/consult-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/consult.webp 960w" sizes="100vw" width="960" height="640" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><div class="phero-shade"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><a href="/policyholder-guides/">Guides</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">Who Should You Call: Appraiser, Adjuster or Attorney?</span></nav><p class="kicker">Guide · 7 min read · August 8, 2026</p><h1>Who Should You Call: Appraiser, Adjuster or Attorney?</h1><p class="lede">Three different professionals help New York policyholders with disputed claims. Here is how appraiser vs public adjuster vs attorney roles compare and when each fits.</p></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>Three roles, often confused</h2>
<p>When a property claim goes sideways, New York policyholders often hear three suggestions at once: hire an appraiser, call a public adjuster, or get a lawyer. The appraiser vs public adjuster question, and where an attorney fits, comes down to what kind of dispute you have and what stage the claim is in. Each role is different, and in some claims more than one makes sense.</p>

<h2>Insurance appraiser</h2>
<p>An appraiser in this sense is appointed under the policy’s appraisal clause after a written demand. Appraisal is a valuation process: it sets the amount of loss when the carrier has accepted a claim but the two sides disagree on the number.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Best fit:</strong> covered claims with a clear gap in price, quantity or scope</li>
<li><strong>Works within:</strong> the appraisal procedure, with a panel and an umpire</li>
<li><strong>Does not do:</strong> decide coverage, give legal advice or negotiate the whole claim</li>
</ul>
<p>Appraisers are expected to be competent and disinterested. Some act as umpires as well; our <a href="/claim-appraisals/appraisal-panel-representation/">appraisal panel representation</a> page describes the role in more detail.</p>

<h2>Public adjuster</h2>
<p>A public adjuster represents the policyholder in preparing, presenting and negotiating the claim with the insurer. In New York, public adjusters are licensed by the Department of Financial Services, and their contracts and fees are subject to state rules.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Best fit:</strong> early in a claim, especially large or complex losses where you want someone to manage documentation and negotiation</li>
<li><strong>Typical fee:</strong> a percentage of the recovery, within limits set by New York rules</li>
<li><strong>Does not do:</strong> give legal advice or file lawsuits</li>
</ul>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Check licensing</b>Before signing with a public adjuster, confirm the license with DFS, read the contract carefully, and ask how the fee is calculated, including on amounts the insurer has already offered.</div>

<h2>Attorney</h2>
<p>A New York attorney experienced in insurance disputes can advise on coverage, policy interpretation, deadlines and remedies, and can file suit when necessary.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Best fit:</strong> denials, coverage disputes, approaching suit-limitation deadlines, or claim-handling concerns</li>
<li><strong>Fee:</strong> varies, including hourly and contingency arrangements</li>
<li><strong>Can do:</strong> what neither appraisers nor public adjusters can, such as giving legal advice and litigating</li>
</ul>
<p>Many New York property policies limit suits to two years from the loss. If that date is approaching, talk to an attorney promptly, whatever other help you have.</p>

<h2>Side-by-side comparison</h2>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Appraiser</th><th>Public adjuster</th><th>Attorney</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Decides coverage?</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>Advises; courts decide</td></tr>
<tr><td>Negotiates the whole claim?</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Values the amount of loss?</td><td>Yes, on a panel</td><td>Prepares the claim value</td><td>Often relies on experts</td></tr>
<tr><td>Gives legal advice?</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>How to choose</h2>
<h3>Claim denied or partly denied</h3>
<p>Start with an attorney. Appraisal generally cannot resolve whether a loss is covered.</p>
<h3>Claim accepted, number too low</h3>
<p>Appraisal may be the most direct route, especially when you already have a solid contractor estimate. A <a href="/claim-appraisals/carrier-estimate-review/">carrier estimate review</a> can show where the gap comes from before you demand appraisal.</p>
<h3>Claim just opened and complex</h3>
<p>A public adjuster may help organize and present the claim from the start. If a value dispute remains later, appraisal is still available under most policies.</p>

<h3>Questions to ask before you hire anyone</h3>
<ul>
<li>What exactly will you do, and what will you not do?</li>
<li>How is your fee calculated, and is it in a written agreement?</li>
<li>Have you worked for my insurer, my contractor or the other side before?</li>
<li>Who else may I need, and when should I bring them in?</li>
<li>What deadlines in my policy should I be watching?</li>
</ul>
<p>Clear answers to these questions help you compare professionals fairly. Be cautious with anyone who promises a specific result, discourages you from reading your policy, or asks you to sign before explaining the fee. A good professional in any of the three roles should be comfortable telling you when someone else is better suited to the problem.</p>

<h2>Combining roles</h2>
<p>These roles can overlap. A public adjuster may recommend appraisal and help prepare for it; an attorney may handle coverage while appraisal resolves the amount. What matters is that each person’s role and fee are clear in writing, so you are not paying twice for the same work. If you are unsure which route fits your New York claim, <a href="/reach-us/">contact us</a> and we will tell you plainly whether appraisal is the right tool.</p>
